d defects. I'm noting solutions for slag entrapment, spatter (including current reduction), and various cracks. I'm also including detailed remedies for lack of fusion, which involves increasing heat, correcting electrode angle, and ensuring a clean surface.\n\n\n**Categorizing Welding Techniques**\n\nI am now organizing welding techniques into distinct categories: fusion welding and pressure welding. I'm noting the subdivisions within each, such as gas welding, arc welding, and their variations. I am also classifying allied processes like brazing and soldering. Furthermore, I am noting the importance of a clean tree diagram for GTU examiners.\n\n\n**Describing Core Placement**\n\nI'm now detailing core placement and support mechanisms within the mould. I've categorized core types based on their position: horizontal, vertical, balanced, hanging, and drop cores. My current focus is on clearly defining the core print and its function in creating the necessary seat or recess for core positioning. I'm also describing the purpose and use of chaplets, particularly when core prints are insufficient. I'm noting the need for a metal support that will fuse with the casting. Finally, I'm working on sketching a diagram to visually explain the principles I've noted.\n\n\n**Summarizing the Steps**\n\nI'm now detailing the steps in sand casting. The ordered sequence includes: pattern making, mould preparation, core setting (when applicable), mould closing, pouring molten metal, solidification, shakeout, and finally, cleaning and finishing the casting. I'm focusing on the critical aspects of each step to achieve a comprehensive overview.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":false,"Summary":"Updated Predicted Questions text file. Stripped out medium and low probability topics. Contains only the top 5 high-probability Tier 2 questions (asked 3-4 times in PYQs).","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"PREDICTED ADDITIONAL EXAM QUESTIONS — BEYOND TIER 1\n====================================================\n(Only High Probability Tier 2 Topics)\n\n=====================================================================\nHIGH PROBABILITY — TIER 2 (Asked 3-4 times in old papers)\n=====================================================================\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n1. PROPERTIES OF MOULDING SAND (4-7 Marks)\nAsked: 3/9 papers\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nQuestion: Explain the properties of moulding sand.\n\nAnswer (Write 6-7 properties for 7M, any 4 for 4M):\n\n1. Permeability: The ability of moulding sand to allow gases and steam to pass through it during pouring. If permeability is low, gases get trapped and cause blow holes in the casting.\n\n2. Flowability (Plasticity): The ability of sand to flow around the pattern and take the exact shape of the mould cavity. Better flowability = better reproduction of fine details.\n\n3. Collapsibility: The ability of the mould to collapse (break down) easily after the casting solidifies. If sand does not collapse, the casting cannot shrink freely and internal stresses or cracks develop.\n\n4. Cohesiveness (Strength): The ability of sand grains to stick together. Without cohesiveness, the mould walls will crumble when molten metal is poured.\n\n5. Refractoriness: The ability of sand to withstand high temperatures of molten metal without fusing or melting. Silica sand has high refractoriness (~1700 deg C).\n\n6. Green Strength: The strength of the mould in its wet (green) condition before drying. Must be enough to hold its shape during handling and core setting.\n\n7. Adhesiveness: The ability of sand to stick to the walls of the moulding flask. Without this, the sand falls away from the flask during mould handling.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n2. WELDING DEFECTS — CAUSES AND REMEDIES (4-7 Marks)\nAsked: 3/9 papers\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nQuestion: Explain various welding defects with their causes and remedies.\n\nAnswer (Write 5-6 defects for 7M):\n\n1. POROSITY (Gas Holes)\n   Cause: Gases trapped in the weld pool due to moisture, grease, or contaminated electrode.\n   Remedy: Clean the base metal properly. Use dry electrodes. Ensure proper shielding gas flow.\n\n2. UNDERCUT\n   Cause: Excessive welding current or too high arc length. A groove forms along the weld toe.\n   Remedy: Reduce current. Use correct arc length. Use proper weaving technique.\n\n3. INCOMPLETE PENETRATION\n   Cause: Low welding current, too fast travel speed, or too large root gap.\n   Remedy: Increase current. Reduce travel speed. Use proper joint design with correct root gap.\n\n4. SLAG INCLUSION\n   Cause: Slag from the previous pass is not cleaned before the next pass is deposited.\n   Remedy: Thoroughly chip and wire-brush slag between every pass.\n\n5. SPATTER\n   Cause: Excessive current, long arc, or wrong polarity.\n   Remedy: Reduce current. Shorten arc length. Use correct polarity for the electrode type.\n\n6. CRACK (Hot or Cold)\n   Cause: Hot cracks — high sulphur/phosphorus in base metal. Cold cracks — hydrogen embrittlement, rapid cooling.\n   Remedy: Use low-hydrogen electrodes. Pre-heat the base metal. Control cooling rate.\n\n7. LACK OF FUSION\n   Cause: Low heat input, wrong electrode angle, or oxide layer on the surface preventing fusion.\n   Remedy: Increase current. Maintain proper electrode angle. Clean the joint surface.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n3. CLASSIFICATION OF WELDING PROCESSES (3-4 Marks)\nAsked: 3/9 papers\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nQuestion: Classify welding processes with a neat chart.\n\nAnswer (Draw this tree):\n\nWELDING PROCESSES\n|\n|--- FUSION WELDING (No pressure applied)\n|    |--- Gas Welding (Oxy-Acetylene)\n|    |--- Arc Welding\n|    |    |--- Shielded Metal Arc Welding (SMAW)\n|    |    |--- TIG (GTAW)\n|    |    |--- MIG (GMAW)\n|    |    |--- Submerged Arc Welding (SAW)\n|    |--- Thermit Welding\n|\n|--- PRESSURE WELDING (Pressure applied)\n|    |--- Resistance Welding (Spot, Seam, Projection)\n|    |--- Forge Welding\n|    |--- Friction Welding\n|    |--- Explosive Welding\n|\n|--- ALLIED PROCESSES\n     |--- Brazing\n     |--- Soldering\n\nNote: Draw this as a neat tree diagram. GTU examiners give 1-2 marks just for the diagram being clean.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n4. CORE TYPES, CORE MAKING, CORE PRINTS, CHAPLETS (4 Marks)\nAsked: 3/9 papers\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nQuestion: Explain types of cores with a sketch. / What are core prints and chaplets?\n\nAnswer:\n\nWHAT IS A CORE:\nA core is a pre-formed body of sand placed inside a mould cavity to create internal holes, passages, or hollow sections in the casting.\n\nTYPES OF CORES (based on position):\n1. Horizontal Core — Placed horizontally across the mould cavity.\n2. Vertical Core — Placed vertically, supported by core prints in cope and drag.\n3. Balanced Core — Supported at only one end (cantilevered). Used for blind holes.\n4. Hanging Core — Suspended from the cope (top half). Used for large cavities open at the bottom.\n5. Drop Core — Placed below the parting line, extends into the drag.\n\nCORE PRINT:\nA core print is an extra projection on the pattern that creates a seat or recess in the mould. The core sits in this recess. The core print does NOT appear in the final casting — it only holds the core in position.\n\nCHAPLET:\nA chaplet is a small metallic support placed inside the mould to hold the core in position when core prints alone are not sufficient. Chaplets are made of the same metal as the casting so they melt and fuse into the casting. They are used when the core is very heavy or long and might shift during pouring.\n\nDiagram: Draw a mould cross-section showing a core sitting on core prints, with chaplets supporting it from below.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n5. SAND CASTING STEP-BY-STEP PROCEDURE (3-4 Marks)\nAsked: 4/9 papers\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nQuestion: Explain the sand casting process with steps.\n\nAnswer (8 steps in order):\n\n1. PATTERN MAKING: Create a pattern (replica of the casting with allowances) from wood, metal, or plastic.\n2. MOULD PREPARATION: Place the pattern in a moulding flask. Pack moulding sand around it. Remove the pattern to create the cavity.\n3. CORE SETTING: If internal holes are needed, place the pre-made sand cores inside the cavity using core prints.\n4. MOULD CLOSING: Place the cope (top half) on the drag (bottom half). Align using pins. Clamp or weight down.\n5. POURING: Pour molten metal through the gating system (pouring basin -> sprue -> runner -> gate -> cavity).\n6. SOLIDIFICATION: Allow the metal to cool and solidify inside the mould.\n7. SHAKEOUT: Break the mould to remove the casting. Destroy the sand mould (sand casting = expendable mould).\n8. CLEANING AND FINISHING: Remove gates, risers, fins, and sand. Inspect the casting.","Description":"Trimmed the file to only include the top 5 high-probability questions. Removed all medium and low probability content.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\cc5ae5f0-3c52-483a-b0d6-e323018f022c\\Predicted_Additional_Questions.txt","toolAction":"Trimming to top 5 questions","toolSummary":"Truncating text file"}}]}
