medy: Thoroughly chip and wire-brush slag between every pass.\r\n\r\nSPATTER Cause: Excessive current, long arc, or wrong polarity. Remedy: Reduce current. Shorten arc length. Use correct polarity for the electrode type.\r\n\r\nCRACK (Hot or Cold) Cause: Hot cracks — high sulphur/phosphorus in base metal. Cold cracks — hydrogen embrittlement, rapid cooling. Remedy: Use low-hydrogen electrodes. Pre-heat the base metal. Control cooling rate.\r\n\r\nLACK OF FUSION Cause: Low heat input, wrong electrode angle, or oxide layer on the surface preventing fusion. Remedy: Increase current. Maintain proper electrode angle. Clean the joint surface.\r\nPROPERTIES OF MOULDING SAND: Question: Explain the properties of moulding sand. Answer (Write 6-7 properties for 7M, any 4 for 4M): • Permeability: The ability of moulding sand to allow gases and steam to pass through it during pouring. If permeability is low, gases get trapped and cause blow holes in the casting. • Flowability (Plasticity): The ability of sand to flow around the pattern and take the exact shape of the mould cavity. Better flowability = better reproduction of fine details. • Collapsibility: The ability of the mould to collapse (break down) easily after the casting solidifies. If sand does not collapse, the casting cannot shrink freely and internal stresses or cracks develop. • Cohesiveness (Strength): The ability of sand grains to stick together. Without cohesiveness, the mould walls will crumble when molten metal is poured. • Refractoriness: The ability of sand to withstand high temperatures of molten metal without fusing or melting. Silica sand has high refractoriness (~1700 deg C). • Green Strength: The strength of the mould in its wet (green) condition before drying. Must be enough to hold its shape during handling and core setting. • Adhesiveness: The ability of sand to stick to the walls of the moulding flask. Without this, the sand falls away from the flask during mould handling.\r\n\r\nWELDING DEFECTS — CA