{"step_index":57,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-08T08:08:14Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\nOut of the topics you just pasted, **exactly ONE** strictly requires a full diagram to get marks: **Gating System Elements**. \n\n*(Note: For casting and welding defects, drawing tiny sketches of the defect—like a bubble for porosity or a groove for undercut—will guarantee you don't lose marks, but the Gating System question absolutely depends on a master diagram).*\n\nHere is exactly what the Gating System diagram is, followed by the prompt you can use to generate it.\n\n### What the Diagram Is\nIt is a 2D cross-sectional view of a sand mould. It maps the exact \"plumbing\" system the liquid metal travels through. It starts at the top where the metal is poured, flows down vertically, moves horizontally, enters the cavity, and pushes excess metal up into a reservoir.\n\n### The Prompt to Generate It\nCopy and paste this into an AI image generator (or use it as mental instructions to draw it yourself):\n\n> \"A simple, clear 2D cross-section engineering line drawing of a sand casting gating system. Black and white textbook style. It must show the following connected sequence: A funnel-shaped 'Pouring Basin' at the top, leading down into a vertical, slightly tapered pipe called the 'Sprue'. At the bottom of the sprue is a small cup called the 'Sprue Well'. From the well, a horizontal channel called the 'Runner' extends to the right, ending in a small opening called the 'Gate' (or Ingate). The gate connects directly into a large rectangular 'Mould Cavity'. On top of the mould cavity, there is a vertical open cylinder called the 'Riser'. Add clear text labels with arrows pointing to each of these 7 components.\"","thinking":"**Focusing on Scope**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the request's core. I've defined the scope, focusing solely on the four distinct topics mentioned: Casting Defects, Gating System Elements, Properties of Moulding Sand, and Welding Defects. I will disregard any additional, non-relevant information. I'm making sure I don't go outside these boundaries.\n\n\n**Identifying Diagram Needs**\n\nI'm now identifying when a diagram is required. It's clear that the gating system element explanation definitely demands a diagram, explicitly. Casting defects might benefit from a simple sketch, but it isn't explicitly requested. I will ensure the final output reflects the distinct need for a visual for the gating system. I am following the constraint to not produce diagrams, instead providing a user-friendly prompt.\n\n\n**Refining Diagram Strategy**\n\nI've clarified the diagram strategy. While the gating system still *demands* a full diagram, I've decided that small sketches would be beneficial for explaining welding and casting defects, though they aren't explicitly requested. I'll include prompts to guide the user in generating these diagrams.\n\n\n"}
